-- mobility-vs-flexibility-athletic-performance
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Athletes often use &lsquo;mobility&rsquo; and &lsquo;flexibility&rsquo; as if they meant the same thing. They do not, and the difference matters. Flexibility is how far a muscle can lengthen. Mobility is how well you can actively control a joint through its range. You can be flexible without being mobile, and for performance, mobility is usually what counts.</p><section id="the-difference"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>The Difference, Simply Put</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop"><strong>Flexibility</strong> is passive range: how far a limb can be moved, for example when a partner pushes your leg up in a hamstring stretch. <strong>Mobility</strong> is active range: how far you can move it yourself, with strength and control at every point.</p><p>A sprinter needs to drive the knee high under control. A swimmer needs to rotate the shoulder powerfully through its full arc. In both cases, range you cannot control is range you cannot use, and range without control is where injuries often happen.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Flexibility: how far a muscle can lengthen</li><li>Mobility: how far you can move a joint with control</li><li>Performance depends mostly on mobility</li></ul></div></section><section id="why-it-matters"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Why Athletes Should Care</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Better mobility lets you reach positions efficiently, so power is not lost fighting your own stiffness. It also spreads load across joints more evenly. When one joint lacks mobility, often the hips, ankles or upper back, the body tends to borrow movement from somewhere else, such as the lower back or knees, which can lead to overload.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-builds-both"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>How Yoga Builds Both</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga is unusually good at building mobility because most postures ask you to hold a position using your own strength rather than gravity or a partner. Warrior II demands active hip range; a high lunge asks the hip flexors to lengthen while the legs work; chair pose loads the ankles and hips through depth.</p><p>Longer, gentler holds, such as in Yin or restorative yoga, add passive flexibility. The combination of both is what makes yoga a useful complement to sport.</p></section><section id="a-mobility-sequence"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A 15-Minute Mobility Sequence</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Cat-cow (1 minute)</strong> for the spine</li><li><strong>Thread the needle (1 minute each side)</strong> for upper-back rotation</li><li><strong>Low lunge with active reach (1 minute each side)</strong> for hips</li><li><strong>Warrior II, holding and pulsing slightly deeper (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Deep squat (Malasana, 2 minutes)</strong> for ankles and hips</li><li><strong>Lying figure-four stretch (1 minute each side)</strong></li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Use mobility work as part of your warm-up and save long passive stretches for after training or a separate session.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is stretching before a game bad?</h3><p>Long, passive static stretches right before explosive activity may slightly reduce power for some athletes. Dynamic, active movement is a better warm-up; keep longer holds for later.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How often should athletes do mobility work?</h3><p>Little and often works best: ten to fifteen minutes on most days usually beats one long session a week.</p></section><section id="putting-it-together"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Putting It Together</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Think of flexibility as the raw material and mobility as the usable skill. Yoga trains both, and a short regular practice alongside your sport can help you move more freely and efficiently.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Mobility vs Flexibility for Athletes: Why the Difference Matters', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'mobility-vs-flexibility-athletic-performance' AND `content` LIKE '%The most effective routine is the one you will actually keep%';

-- yoga-for-sports-recovery-muscle-restoration
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Training makes you fitter only if you recover from it. The adaptations happen between sessions, not during them. Many athletes train hard and recover passively, if at all. Yoga offers an active, gentle way to support recovery: easing stiffness, calming the nervous system and helping you notice what your body needs.</p><section id="why-recovery-matters"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Recovery Is Part of Training</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Hard training creates fatigue and small amounts of muscle damage. Given rest, nutrition and sleep, the body repairs and adapts, coming back a little stronger. Without enough recovery, fatigue accumulates, performance stalls, and the risk of injury and illness rises.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Supports Recovery</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Gentle movement</strong> keeps blood flowing to tired muscles without adding load.</li><li><strong>Restoring range</strong> that tightens after hard sessions.</li><li><strong>Calming the nervous system,</strong> helping the body shift into its rest-and-repair mode.</li><li><strong>Better sleep,</strong> the most powerful recovery tool there is.</li><li><strong>Body awareness,</strong> so you notice niggles before they become injuries.</li></ul></section><section id="a-recovery-sequence"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Post-Training Recovery Sequence</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Keep it slow, comfortable and short, around 15 to 20 minutes:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Child&rsquo;s pose (2 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Reclined hamstring stretch with a strap (1 minute each leg)</strong></li><li><strong>Supine twist (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Reclined pigeon / figure-four (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Legs up the wall (5 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Slow breathing,</strong> exhaling longer than you inhale (3 minutes)</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Recovery yoga should feel easy. If you are working hard in it, you are training, not recovering.</p></div></section><section id="timing"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>When to Practise</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Gentle yoga works well later on a training day or on rest days. Straight after very intense sessions, start with a few minutes of easy movement and breathing before any deeper stretching, and avoid pushing into tissue that is already very sore.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga replace a rest day?</h3><p>Gentle, restorative yoga can be part of a rest day. A demanding power class cannot; it is another training session.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Does yoga reduce muscle soreness?</h3><p>Many athletes find gentle movement eases the stiffness of soreness, and it may help them feel better faster. Sleep, food and time remain the main drivers of recovery.</p></section><section id="the-full-picture"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>The Full Recovery Picture</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga works alongside the fundamentals: enough sleep, enough protein and carbohydrate, hydration and sensible training loads. Persistent pain, swelling or pain that changes how you move needs a physiotherapist or doctor.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Adaptation happens during recovery</li><li>Keep recovery yoga gentle</li><li>Sleep and food still come first</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Sports Recovery: Restore Your Body and Come Back Stronger', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-sports-recovery-muscle-restoration' AND `content` LIKE '%Every transition, welcome or not, dissolves familiar structures the nerv%';

-- yoga-for-runners-endurance-athletes
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Running, cycling and other endurance sports repeat the same movement thousands of times. That builds a remarkable engine, and also predictable tightness and imbalances. Yoga is a natural counterweight: it opens what endurance training closes, strengthens what it neglects, and trains the breath and focus that long efforts demand.</p><section id="what-endurance-does"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Endurance Training Does to the Body</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Repetitive, forward-only movement tends to tighten the hip flexors, calves and hamstrings, and can leave the glutes and deep core under-used. Cyclists add a rounded upper back from hours in the saddle. Over time these patterns can change how you move and load your joints.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Helps Endurance Athletes</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Hip mobility</strong> for a fuller, more efficient stride</li><li><strong>Glute and core strength</strong> to support the pelvis and lower back</li><li><strong>Ankle and foot mobility</strong> for better push-off and landing</li><li><strong>Breath control</strong> that helps you stay relaxed at effort</li><li><strong>Mental focus</strong> for the long middle miles</li></ul></section><section id="key-poses"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Key Poses for Runners and Cyclists</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Low lunge</strong> for the hip flexors</li><li><strong>Downward dog</strong> for calves and hamstrings</li><li><strong>Pigeon or figure-four</strong> for the outer hips</li><li><strong>Bridge pose</strong> to wake up the glutes</li><li><strong>Chair pose</strong> for strength through ankles, knees and hips</li><li><strong>Supported backbends</strong> to counter a rounded upper back</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Pair a strength pose with each stretch. Opening the hips without strengthening the glutes leaves the job half done.</p></div></section><section id="breath-and-focus"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Breath and Focus</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Endurance is as much mental as physical. Yoga trains you to stay with the breath and the present moment when things get uncomfortable. Practising slow nasal breathing in easy sessions can help you notice tension and stay relaxed when the effort rises.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should runners do yoga before or after a run?</h3><p>Short, dynamic movement works before a run; longer holds suit after a run or a separate session.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How often should endurance athletes do yoga?</h3><p>Two short sessions a week is a good start, fitted around your hardest training days rather than before them.</p></section><section id="fitting-it-in"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Fitting It Into Your Training</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Treat yoga as part of the plan, not an extra you skip when busy. Twenty minutes twice a week, sustained over a season, can make a noticeable difference to how you move and how you feel.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Open what your sport tightens</li><li>Strengthen what it neglects</li><li>Use the breath to stay relaxed at effort</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Runners and Endurance Athletes: Move Better, Last Longer', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-runners-endurance-athletes' AND `content` LIKE '%Yoga supports lasting growth by creating the conditions it requires%';

-- yoga-for-athletes-beginners-guide-performance-recovery
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">More and more athletes, from weekend footballers to professionals, are adding yoga to their training. If you have never done it before, it can look like a world of unfamiliar names and impossible poses. It does not need to be. This guide covers why athletes do yoga, where to start, and how to fit it in.</p><section id="why-athletes-do-yoga"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Athletes Do Yoga</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Mobility:</strong> moving through a fuller, controlled range</li><li><strong>Balance and stability:</strong> better control of joints under load</li><li><strong>Recovery:</strong> easing stiffness and calming the nervous system</li><li><strong>Breathing:</strong> more efficient, relaxed breathing under effort</li><li><strong>Focus:</strong> staying present under pressure</li><li><strong>Body awareness:</strong> noticing imbalances before they cause problems</li></ul></section><section id="choosing-a-style"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Choosing a Style</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Different styles suit different goals. <strong>Vinyasa</strong> and <strong>power yoga</strong> are dynamic and build strength and stamina. <strong>Hatha</strong> is slower, a good place to learn the basics. <strong>Yin</strong> and <strong>restorative</strong> yoga use long, gentle holds that suit recovery days.</p><p>Many athletes do best with one dynamic session and one gentle session a week.</p></section><section id="where-to-start"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Where to Start</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Begin with a beginner class or a short routine of basic postures: downward dog, low lunge, warrior II, chair pose, bridge, child&rsquo;s pose and a simple twist. Learn them well before adding complexity.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Leave your competitive instinct at the door. Yoga rewards control and patience, not depth. The athlete who forces a pose usually gets less from it than the one who stays within comfortable range.</p></div></section><section id="fitting-it-in"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Fitting It Into a Training Week</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Put dynamic yoga on lighter training days.</li><li>Use gentle yoga on rest days or after hard sessions.</li><li>Avoid long, deep stretching right before explosive training or competition.</li><li>Start with 20 to 30 minutes, twice a week.</li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">I&rsquo;m not flexible. Can I still do yoga?</h3><p>Yes. Stiffness is the reason to start, not a reason to wait. Use blocks and straps, and bend your knees as much as you need.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Will yoga make me lose strength or muscle?</h3><p>No. Yoga complements strength training rather than replacing it, and the more active styles build strength of their own.</p></section><section id="stick-with-it"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Stick With It</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The benefits of yoga build gradually. Give it six to eight weeks of regular practice before judging what it does for you.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Pick a style that matches your goal</li><li>Start simple and stay within range</li><li>Consistency beats intensity</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Athletes: A Beginner''s Guide to Performance and Recovery', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-athletes-beginners-guide-performance-recovery' AND `content` LIKE '%The nervous system responds to the disruption of the familiar rather tha%';

-- how-yoga-helps-prevent-sports-injuries
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">The best injury is the one that never happens. Many sports injuries come from overload, poor control and imbalances that build quietly for weeks before something gives. Yoga cannot make anyone injury-proof, but it trains several of the qualities that help keep athletes on the field.</p><section id="why-injuries-happen"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Injuries Happen</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Common contributors include doing too much too soon, poor recovery, limited mobility in key joints, weak stabilising muscles, and fatigue that erodes technique. Often it is not one thing but several adding up.</p></section><section id="what-yoga-trains"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Yoga Trains</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Joint control:</strong> holding positions under your own strength builds stability through the range.</li><li><strong>Balance and proprioception:</strong> single-leg postures train the reflexes that protect ankles and knees.</li><li><strong>Mobility:</strong> reducing the need to borrow movement from vulnerable joints.</li><li><strong>Core and hip strength:</strong> supporting the spine and pelvis.</li><li><strong>Body awareness:</strong> noticing tightness and asymmetries early.</li></ul><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Stability through range</li><li>Single-leg balance</li><li>Early awareness of problems</li></ul></div></section><section id="key-practices"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Key Practices</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Single-leg balances</strong> such as tree pose and warrior III</li><li><strong>Chair pose and lunges</strong> for controlled strength in the legs</li><li><strong>Plank variations</strong> and side plank for the trunk</li><li><strong>Bridge pose</strong> for the glutes and hamstrings</li><li><strong>Hip and thoracic mobility</strong> with lunges and twists</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Compare left and right sides honestly. A pose that is much harder on one side is useful information worth sharing with your coach or physio.</p></div></section><section id="listen-to-your-body"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Learning to Listen</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">One of yoga&rsquo;s quieter benefits is learning the difference between effort and pain. Athletes are trained to push through discomfort, which is useful in competition and risky in training. Yoga practises noticing sensations without immediately overriding them.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga prevent all injuries?</h3><p>No. Contact, accidents and overtraining can still cause injury. Yoga reduces some risk factors; sensible training load and recovery matter just as much.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I do yoga when I&rsquo;m injured?</h3><p>Only with guidance. Some gentle practice may help, but check with a physiotherapist and avoid anything that aggravates the injury.</p></section><section id="part-of-a-plan"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Part of a Bigger Plan</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga works best alongside sensible training progression, strength work, sleep and good nutrition. Any pain that persists, worsens or changes how you move deserves a professional assessment.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'How Yoga Helps Prevent Sports Injuries Before They Start', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'how-yoga-helps-prevent-sports-injuries' AND `content` LIKE '%When the body finally relaxes, emotions that were held on hold can surfa%';

-- yoga-vs-gym-workouts
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">It is one of the most common fitness questions: should I do yoga or go to the gym? The honest answer is that it depends on what you want, and for most people the best answer is a bit of both. Here is how they compare and how to choose.</p><section id="what-the-gym-does-best"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What the Gym Does Best</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Building strength and muscle,</strong> especially with progressive weight training</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular fitness</strong> through running, rowing or cycling</li><li><strong>Measurable progress:</strong> heavier weights, faster times</li><li><strong>Bone health,</strong> from heavy loading</li></ul></section><section id="what-yoga-does-best"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Yoga Does Best</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Mobility and flexibility</strong></li><li><strong>Balance and body control</strong></li><li><strong>Stress reduction and better sleep</strong></li><li><strong>Breathing and focus</strong></li><li><strong>Bodyweight strength</strong> and endurance in the more dynamic styles</li></ul><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Gym: strength, muscle, cardio</li><li>Yoga: mobility, balance, calm</li><li>Both: better health when combined</li></ul></div></section><section id="by-goal"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Choosing by Goal</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Build muscle:</strong> the gym first, yoga for mobility and recovery.</li><li><strong>Lose weight:</strong> whatever you will do consistently, plus attention to diet. Both can help.</li><li><strong>Reduce stress:</strong> yoga has the edge.</li><li><strong>Move better and ease stiffness:</strong> yoga.</li><li><strong>Sports performance:</strong> a combination, built around your sport.</li><li><strong>Healthy ageing:</strong> both, since strength, balance and mobility all decline without use.</li></ul></section><section id="combining-them"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Combining Them</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A simple weekly pattern for many people: two or three gym sessions and one or two yoga sessions. Use yoga on lighter days or as a cool-down, and avoid a hard yoga session right before heavy leg training.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>The best programme is the one you enjoy enough to keep doing. Consistency over months beats the perfect plan you abandon in three weeks.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga build muscle?</h3><p>Dynamic styles build strength and some muscle endurance, especially for beginners, but heavy weight training is more effective for building muscle size.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is yoga enough exercise on its own?</h3><p>Active yoga practised regularly can meet a lot of fitness needs. Adding some brisk cardio and heavier strength work covers the rest.</p></section><section id="the-verdict"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>The Verdict</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga and the gym are not rivals. They train different things, and together they cover almost everything the body needs. Start with what you enjoy, and add the other to fill the gaps.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga vs Gym: Which Is Better for Your Fitness Goals?', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-vs-gym-workouts' AND `content` LIKE '%A short morning practice works on several systems at once%';

-- yoga-for-strength-independence-functional-movement-seniors
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Independence in later life often comes down to simple things: getting up from a chair, climbing stairs, carrying shopping, getting down to the floor and back up. These depend on strength, and strength fades without use. The good news is that muscles respond to training at any age, and yoga is a gentle, adaptable way to keep them working.</p><section id="strength-and-ageing"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Strength Matters More With Age</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Muscle mass and strength naturally decline with age, particularly if we become less active. Loss of leg strength in particular makes everyday tasks harder and raises the risk of falls. Regular strength work can slow this decline and, for many people, reverse some of it.</p></section><section id="functional-movement"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Functional Movement</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Functional movement means training the movements you actually use in daily life: sitting and standing, stepping, reaching, bending, turning and lifting. Many yoga postures map directly onto these:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Chair pose</strong> trains the strength to sit and stand</li><li><strong>Lunges and warrior poses</strong> train stepping and stair climbing</li><li><strong>Standing balances</strong> train stability while walking and turning</li><li><strong>Gentle backbends</strong> support upright posture</li><li><strong>Moving from floor to standing</strong> trains a skill worth keeping</li></ul></section><section id="a-simple-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Simple Strength Routine</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Sit-to-stand from a chair (8 to 10 times)</strong></li><li><strong>Supported chair pose,</strong> hands on a chair back (3 holds of 20 seconds)</li><li><strong>Warrior II with a chair for support (30 seconds each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Wall push-ups (8 to 10)</strong></li><li><strong>Bridge pose on the floor or bed (5 slow lifts)</strong></li><li><strong>Gentle twist and rest</strong></li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you have heart disease, high blood pressure, osteoporosis, joint replacements or balance problems, check with your doctor before starting, and tell your teacher so poses can be adapted.</p></div></section><section id="progressing"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Progressing Safely</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Start with what feels manageable and build gradually: more repetitions, slightly longer holds, less support from the chair. Mild muscle effort is good; sharp pain is a signal to stop.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is it too late to build strength in my 70s or 80s?</h3><p>No. Muscles respond to training throughout life, and older adults often see meaningful improvements in strength and everyday function with regular practice.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How often should I practise?</h3><p>Two or three sessions a week, with some daily movement like walking, is a good aim.</p></section><section id="staying-independent"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Staying Independent</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Strength is one of the best investments in independence. Pair yoga with walking and, if possible, some other strength training, and keep at it.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Strength fades without use, and returns with it</li><li>Train the movements of daily life</li><li>Start supported and progress gradually</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Strength and Independence: Stay Strong as You Age', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-strength-independence-functional-movement-seniors' AND `content` LIKE '%The digital environment is designed to harvest attention, offering a ste%';

-- yoga-for-balance-fall-prevention-seniors
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Falls are one of the biggest threats to health and independence in later life, and fear of falling can quietly shrink a person&rsquo;s world. Balance, though, is a skill, and like any skill it can be trained. Yoga is one of the gentlest and most effective ways to do it.</p><section id="why-balance-declines"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Balance Declines</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Balance relies on strength in the legs and hips, flexibility in the ankles, good vision, the inner ear and the body&rsquo;s sense of position. These can all decline with age and inactivity. Some medicines can also affect balance, so it is worth asking your doctor or pharmacist about yours.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Trains Balance</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Leg and hip strength</strong> to hold you steady</li><li><strong>Ankle mobility</strong> for quick, small corrections</li><li><strong>Body awareness,</strong> sharpening your sense of where you are in space</li><li><strong>Confidence,</strong> practising balance safely so fear eases</li><li><strong>Posture,</strong> keeping your centre of gravity over your feet</li></ul></section><section id="balance-practices"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Balance Practices to Try</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Always practise next to a wall, counter or sturdy chair:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Mountain pose:</strong> stand tall, weight even across both feet.</li><li><strong>Weight shifts:</strong> slowly move your weight from one foot to the other.</li><li><strong>Heel raises:</strong> rise onto your toes and lower slowly.</li><li><strong>Tree pose with the toes on the floor,</strong> then lifting the foot to the ankle as you improve.</li><li><strong>Heel-to-toe walking</strong> along a counter.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you have heart disease, high blood pressure, osteoporosis, joint replacements or balance problems, check with your doctor before starting, and tell your teacher so poses can be adapted.</p></div></section><section id="make-your-home-safer"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Make Your Home Safer Too</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Balance training works best alongside practical steps: good lighting, removing loose rugs, grab rails in the bathroom, well-fitting shoes, and regular eye checks.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How long before my balance improves?</h3><p>Many people notice they feel steadier within a few weeks of regular practice. Keep going: the benefits fade if you stop.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">I&rsquo;ve already had a fall. Should I still do yoga?</h3><p>Often yes, and it can help rebuild confidence, but speak to your doctor first and start with chair-based or well-supported practice.</p></section><section id="little-and-often"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Little and Often</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A few minutes of balance practice most days is more valuable than one long session a week. Build it into routines, such as standing on one foot, near the counter, while the kettle boils.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Balance is a trainable skill</li><li>Always practise with support nearby</li><li>Daily practice beats occasional effort</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Balance and Fall Prevention in Older Adults', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-balance-fall-prevention-seniors' AND `content` LIKE '%Social media generates strain through three main channels%';

-- yoga-for-joint-mobility-flexibility-age
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Stiff in the morning, slower to get going, a bit more effort to bend down or look over your shoulder: joint stiffness is one of the most familiar parts of getting older. Some of it is ageing, but much of it is simply the result of moving less. Gentle, regular movement is one of the best remedies there is.</p><section id="why-joints-stiffen"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Joints Stiffen</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Joints are kept healthy by movement. Movement helps circulate the fluid that nourishes cartilage and keeps surrounding tissues supple. When we move less, joints tend to lose range, muscles tighten, and stiffness builds, which makes moving feel harder, and so we move even less.</p><p>Conditions such as osteoarthritis also become more common with age. Yoga cannot reverse joint damage, but appropriate movement is widely recommended to manage stiffness and maintain function.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Helps</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga takes each joint gently through its range, builds supporting strength, and encourages relaxation, which can reduce the muscle guarding that adds to stiffness. Because it is slow and adaptable, it suits stiff bodies well.</p></section><section id="a-joint-friendly-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Joint-Friendly Routine</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Neck:</strong> slow turns and tilts</li><li><strong>Shoulders:</strong> circles and gentle arm raises</li><li><strong>Spine:</strong> seated cat-cow and gentle twists in a chair</li><li><strong>Hips:</strong> seated knee lifts and gentle circles</li><li><strong>Knees:</strong> seated leg extensions</li><li><strong>Ankles and wrists:</strong> circles in both directions</li></ul><p>Move slowly, breathe steadily and stay within a comfortable range. Mild stretching sensation is fine; pain is not.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Stiffness is often worst first thing. A few minutes of gentle movement before getting out of bed, or after a warm shower, can make mornings much easier.</p></div></section><section id="arthritis"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>If You Have Arthritis</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Gentle movement usually helps, but during flare-ups keep it very light. Avoid pushing painful joints into deep positions, and use props such as cushions and chairs freely. A physiotherapist can advise on movements that suit your joints.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga cure arthritis?</h3><p>No, but regular gentle movement can ease stiffness, maintain range and support the muscles around the joint.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is chair yoga as good as mat yoga?</h3><p>For mobility, chair yoga can be very effective and is much easier for many older adults to do safely.</p></section><section id="keep-moving"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Keep Moving</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The simplest rule for stiff joints is to move them a little, often. A short daily routine done consistently does more than an occasional long session.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Movement nourishes joints</li><li>Stay within comfortable range</li><li>A little every day</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Joint Mobility: Ease Age-Related Stiffness Naturally', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-joint-mobility-flexibility-age' AND `content` LIKE '%By activating the parasympathetic branch through breath, movement and st%';

-- yoga-for-seniors-a-beginner-friendly-guide-to-getting-started
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">It is never too late to start yoga. Many people take their first class in their sixties, seventies or beyond, and find it becomes one of the most rewarding parts of their week. You do not need to be flexible, fit or young. You only need a willingness to begin gently.</p><section id="benefits"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Start Now</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Better balance</strong> and lower risk of falls</li><li><strong>Stronger legs and core</strong> for everyday tasks</li><li><strong>Easier movement</strong> and less stiffness</li><li><strong>Calmer mind</strong> and better sleep</li><li><strong>Social connection,</strong> if you join a class</li></ul></section><section id="before-you-begin"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Before You Begin</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Talk to your doctor if you have heart or blood pressure problems, osteoporosis, joint replacements, glaucoma, or have had recent surgery or falls. These rarely rule yoga out, but they may change what is safe for you.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Look for classes described as gentle, beginner, chair yoga or yoga for seniors, and tell the teacher about any health conditions before class.</p></div></section><section id="what-to-expect"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>What to Expect From a Class</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A typical gentle class includes some breathing, slow warm-up movements, a few standing or seated postures, and a relaxation at the end. Good teachers offer options for different abilities, and using a chair, blocks or a wall is normal and encouraged.</p></section><section id="a-first-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Gentle First Routine at Home</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Seated breathing (2 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Seated cat-cow (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Shoulder rolls and arm raises (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Seated twist (30 seconds each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Standing mountain pose holding a chair (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Heel raises at the chair (10 times)</strong></li><li><strong>Rest with eyes closed (3 minutes)</strong></li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">I can&rsquo;t get down to the floor. Can I still do yoga?</h3><p>Yes. Chair yoga offers most of the benefits without getting down to the floor, and many classes are entirely chair-based.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How long should a session be?</h3><p>Fifteen to thirty minutes is plenty to start with. Build up gradually as you feel ready.</p></section><section id="go-gently"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Go Gently, Keep Going</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Move within comfort, breathe steadily, and rest whenever you need to. Progress may be slow, but it is real, and it adds up.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>You are never too old to start</li><li>Chairs and props are part of the practice</li><li>Gentle and regular beats ambitious</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Seniors: A Beginner-Friendly Guide to Getting Started', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-seniors-a-beginner-friendly-guide-to-getting-started' AND `content` LIKE '%Anchor small actions to existing routines rather than relying on willpow%';

-- yoga-for-healthy-aging-longevity-guide
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Healthy ageing is not about avoiding birthdays. It is about keeping the ability to do what matters to you: walking with friends, playing with grandchildren, travelling, looking after yourself. Movement is central to that, and yoga offers a way to keep moving well that adapts to you at every age.</p><section id="what-changes"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Changes With Age</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Over the decades, strength, balance, flexibility and bone density tend to decline, especially with inactivity. Recovery takes longer, and stiffness can creep in. These changes are partly inevitable, but the rate at which they happen is strongly influenced by how much we move.</p></section><section id="four-pillars"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>The Four Pillars of Moving Well</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Strength:</strong> to stand, climb and carry</li><li><strong>Balance:</strong> to stay steady and avoid falls</li><li><strong>Mobility:</strong> to bend, reach and turn comfortably</li><li><strong>Endurance:</strong> to keep going through the day</li></ul><p>Yoga trains the first three directly and supports the fourth. Pairing it with walking or another aerobic activity covers the rest.</p></section><section id="by-decade"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Adapting Practice Through the Decades</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>40s and 50s:</strong> keep strength and mobility up; this is a great time to build habits.</li><li><strong>60s:</strong> prioritise balance and leg strength; adapt poses around any joint issues.</li><li><strong>70s and beyond:</strong> chair-supported practice, balance work and gentle mobility every day.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>The practice should change as you do. A practice you can keep doing for decades is worth more than an impressive one you cannot sustain.</p></div></section><section id="mind-and-mood"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Mind and Mood Matter Too</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Healthy ageing includes mental wellbeing. Yoga&rsquo;s breathing and relaxation can help with stress and sleep, and group classes offer connection, which matters for wellbeing at any age.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga help me live longer?</h3><p>Regular physical activity is strongly linked with better health in later life. Yoga is one enjoyable way to stay active; the key is keeping it up.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">What is the single most important thing to train as I age?</h3><p>Leg strength and balance are hard to beat, because they underpin independence and help prevent falls.</p></section><section id="start-where-you-are"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Start Where You Are</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Whatever your age, the best time to begin is now, and the best practice is one you will keep. Check with your doctor if you have health conditions, and build up gradually.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Strength, balance, mobility, endurance</li><li>Adapt the practice as you age</li><li>Consistency over decades</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Healthy Aging: How to Keep Moving Well at Every Age', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-healthy-aging-longevity-guide' AND `content` LIKE '%Through the day, take two to three minutes of palming and blinking every%';

-- yoga-for-healthy-aging-longevity-senior-wellness
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Senior wellness is about more than avoiding illness. It covers how well you move, how well you sleep, how connected you feel and how much you enjoy daily life. Yoga touches all of these, which is part of why so many older adults make it a lasting habit.</p><section id="whole-person"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Wellness for the Whole Person</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Good health in later life rests on several foundations: staying physically active, eating well, sleeping well, managing stress, staying socially connected and keeping the mind engaged. Yoga contributes to many of them at once.</p></section><section id="physical"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Physical Wellness</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Regular yoga builds strength and balance, keeps joints moving and supports good posture. For many older adults, it also makes other activities, from walking to gardening, easier and more enjoyable.</p></section><section id="mental-and-social"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Mental and Social Wellness</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Slow breathing and relaxation can help calm the mind and improve sleep. Learning new sequences keeps the brain engaged. And a regular class offers friendship and routine, which can be especially valuable for people living alone.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;A yoga class can be as much about the people you meet there as the poses you learn.&rdquo;</p></div></section><section id="a-weekly-rhythm"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Healthy Weekly Rhythm</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Two or three yoga sessions,</strong> gentle or chair-based as needed</li><li><strong>Daily walking</strong> or other light activity</li><li><strong>Balance practice</strong> for a few minutes most days</li><li><strong>A short relaxation or breathing practice</strong> in the evening</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you have heart disease, high blood pressure, osteoporosis, joint replacements or balance problems, check with your doctor before starting, and tell your teacher so poses can be adapted.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Which yoga style is best for seniors?</h3><p>Gentle Hatha, chair yoga and restorative yoga are usually the best places to start. Look for teachers experienced with older students.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga help with memory?</h3><p>Regular physical activity and staying engaged are associated with better brain health. Yoga offers both, though it is not a treatment for memory conditions.</p></section><section id="enjoy-it"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Make It Enjoyable</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The secret of longevity in any habit is enjoyment. Find a teacher, a class or a routine you look forward to, and wellness tends to follow.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Healthy Aging, Longevity and Senior Wellness', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-healthy-aging-longevity-senior-wellness' AND `content` LIKE '%Being reachable everywhere sounds like freedom, but it keeps the nervous%';

-- aging-myths-exercise-yoga-seniors
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Many older adults hold back from exercise because of beliefs that simply are not true. Some come from outdated advice, some from fear, and some from comparing themselves to younger bodies. Here are the most common myths, and what is closer to the truth.</p><section id="myth-too-old"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Myth: &ldquo;I&rsquo;m Too Old to Start&rdquo;</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Bodies remain responsive to exercise throughout life. Older adults who start strength and balance training often see meaningful improvements in strength, steadiness and confidence. Starting late still brings real benefits.</p></section><section id="myth-rest"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Myth: &ldquo;At My Age, I Should Rest&rdquo;</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Rest has its place, but too much inactivity speeds up the loss of strength, balance and mobility. For most older adults, the bigger risk is moving too little rather than too much. The key is choosing the right kind and amount of activity.</p></section><section id="myth-flexible"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Myth: &ldquo;Yoga Is Only for Flexible People&rdquo;</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Flexibility is a result of yoga, not a requirement. Good classes for older adults use chairs, blocks and walls so that everyone can take part safely.</p></section><section id="myth-falls"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Myth: &ldquo;Exercise Will Make Me More Likely to Fall&rdquo;</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Appropriate balance and strength training is one of the most effective ways to reduce falls. Practising with support nearby builds stability and confidence rather than risk.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>You can start at any age</li><li>Too little movement is the bigger risk</li><li>Flexibility is earned, not required</li><li>Balance training reduces falls</li></ul></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Do I need to be fit before I start yoga?</h3><p>No. Gentle and chair-based yoga are designed for people starting from wherever they are.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is it dangerous to exercise with a heart condition or high blood pressure?</h3><p>Often it is beneficial, but you should get your doctor&rsquo;s advice first about what is safe for you, and avoid straining or holding your breath.</p></section><section id="what-is-true"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>What Is True</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Some things genuinely do change with age: recovery takes longer and some movements need adapting. That is a reason to train sensibly, not a reason to stop. Check with your doctor if you have health conditions, then start gently and build up.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Find a teacher who works with older students. The right guidance makes it far easier to start safely and keep going.</p></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Aging Myths About Exercise and Yoga: What Actually Holds True', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'aging-myths-exercise-yoga-seniors' AND `content` LIKE '%Constant connectivity keeps the nervous system in low%';

-- complete-yoga-fitness-routine-strength-flexibility
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Most people think of yoga as stretching. Done well, it is just as much a strength practice. The real value is that it builds both at the same time: strength through the range you are gaining, which is what makes flexibility useful and safe. Here is a complete routine that trains both.</p><section id="why-both"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Train Both Together</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Flexibility without strength can leave joints loose and poorly controlled. Strength without flexibility can leave you powerful but stiff. Training them together, as yoga does, builds range you can actually use, whether in sport, the gym or daily life.</p></section><section id="the-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>The Routine (About 35 Minutes)</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop"><strong>Warm-up (5 minutes):</strong> cat-cow, gentle lunges and three slow sun salutations.</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Chair pose (3 × 30 seconds)</strong> for legs and back</li><li><strong>Warrior II to reverse warrior (5 rounds each side)</strong></li><li><strong>High lunge with a twist (30 seconds each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Plank to low plank, slowly (5 times, knees down if needed)</strong></li><li><strong>Side plank (20 seconds each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Bridge pose (5 slow lifts, then a 30-second hold)</strong></li><li><strong>Pigeon or figure-four (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Seated forward fold (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Supine twist and relaxation (5 minutes)</strong></li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Move slowly in the strength poses. Control is what builds strength; speed and momentum mostly hide weakness.</p></div></section><section id="how-often"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>How Often to Do It</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Two or three times a week is enough to see progress in both strength and flexibility over six to eight weeks. On other days, a short ten-minute mobility routine keeps the gains coming.</p></section><section id="progress"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>How to Progress</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Hold strength poses a little longer, lower yourself more slowly, and reduce support as you get stronger. For flexibility, breathe into stretches and let range come gradually rather than forcing it.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Strength through range</li><li>Slow and controlled beats fast</li><li>Progress gradually over weeks</li></ul></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga replace weight training?</h3><p>It builds good bodyweight strength, especially for beginners, but heavier lifting is more effective for building maximal strength and muscle. Many people combine the two.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I stretch every day?</h3><p>Gentle daily mobility is fine for most people. Deep, intense stretching benefits from rest days in between.</p></section><section id="make-it-yours"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Make It Yours</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Adapt the routine to your body and goals. If you sit a lot, spend more time on hips and upper back; if you lift, add more mobility for the shoulders and hips.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Strength and Flexibility: A Complete Routine for Both', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'complete-yoga-fitness-routine-strength-flexibility' AND `content` LIKE '%A blocked heart centre can look like chronic loneliness even inside a fu%';

-- yoga-for-recovery-muscle-relaxation
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">The day or two after a hard workout, everything aches: stairs feel steeper and sitting down is an event. Delayed-onset muscle soreness is normal, but it does not mean you have to sit still. Gentle yoga can ease the stiffness, help you move more comfortably and support the recovery that makes training worthwhile.</p><section id="why-muscles-get-sore"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Muscles Get Sore</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Soreness usually peaks a day or two after unfamiliar or intense exercise, especially movements that lengthen a muscle under load, like running downhill or lowering heavy weights. It is part of the body adapting and normally fades within a few days.</p></section><section id="gentle-movement-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Why Gentle Movement Helps</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Complete rest can leave sore muscles feeling even stiffer. Light movement increases blood flow, eases stiffness and helps you feel better, even though it does not speed up the repair process dramatically. Yoga adds slow breathing and relaxation, helping the nervous system settle too.</p></section><section id="a-recovery-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A 20-Minute Recovery Routine</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Child&rsquo;s pose (2 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Cat-cow (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Low lunge, gently (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Reclined hamstring stretch with a strap (1 minute each leg)</strong></li><li><strong>Figure-four stretch (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Supine twist (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Legs up the wall (5 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Rest with slow breathing (3 minutes)</strong></li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Stretch to mild sensation only. Sore muscles do not need to be pushed; they need to be moved.</p></div></section><section id="other-recovery-tools"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Other Recovery Essentials</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Sleep:</strong> the most important recovery tool of all</li><li><strong>Food:</strong> enough protein and carbohydrate after training</li><li><strong>Hydration</strong></li><li><strong>Sensible training:</strong> increase intensity gradually to limit severe soreness</li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I do yoga when I&rsquo;m sore?</h3><p>Gentle yoga usually helps. Avoid intense or deep stretching on very sore muscles, and keep the session easy.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How do I tell soreness from injury?</h3><p>Soreness is a general, dull ache across a muscle that eases with gentle movement over a few days. Sharp pain, swelling, pain in a joint or pain that gets worse should be checked by a professional.</p></section><section id="listen-and-adjust"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Listen and Adjust</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Use recovery sessions to notice how your body feels. Over time you will learn what helps you bounce back fastest.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Move gently, don&rsquo;t rest completely</li><li>Mild stretches only</li><li>Sleep and food do the heavy lifting</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Recovery: Reduce Soreness and Restore Movement', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-recovery-muscle-relaxation' AND `content` LIKE '%Trauma, especially anything touching on safety or survival, tends to lod%';

-- yoga-for-flexibility-and-mobility
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Plenty of people stretch for years without getting noticeably more flexible. Usually it is not because their body cannot change, but because of how they stretch: too rarely, too briefly or too forcefully. Here is what actually helps improve range of motion, and how yoga fits in.</p><section id="what-limits-flexibility"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Limits Flexibility</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Range of motion is limited by muscle and connective tissue, joint structure and, importantly, the nervous system. Much of a stretch&rsquo;s &lsquo;stopping point&rsquo; is the nervous system protecting you. Improving flexibility is partly about teaching it that a new range is safe.</p></section><section id="what-works"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Actually Works</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Consistency:</strong> stretching most days beats occasional long sessions.</li><li><strong>Time under stretch:</strong> holding stretches for around 30 to 60 seconds, repeated.</li><li><strong>Relaxation:</strong> slow breathing helps muscles release.</li><li><strong>Strength at the end of range:</strong> active stretching makes the new range usable.</li><li><strong>Patience:</strong> meaningful changes take weeks to months.</li></ul><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Little and often</li><li>Breathe and relax into it</li><li>Strengthen the range you gain</li></ul></div></section><section id="key-areas"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Key Areas and Poses</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Hamstrings:</strong> downward dog, reclined hamstring stretch with a strap</li><li><strong>Hips:</strong> low lunge, pigeon, butterfly</li><li><strong>Spine:</strong> cat-cow, twists, sphinx</li><li><strong>Shoulders:</strong> thread the needle, puppy pose</li><li><strong>Calves and ankles:</strong> downward dog with alternating heels, deep squat</li></ul></section><section id="dont-force-it"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Don&rsquo;t Force It</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Forcing a stretch tends to trigger the body&rsquo;s protective tightening, and it risks strain. Aim for a clear stretching sensation, not pain, and let your breath lead. Warm muscles also stretch more comfortably, so stretch after a short warm-up or at the end of activity.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Track one or two simple measures, like how far you reach in a forward fold, every couple of weeks. Progress is easier to see than to feel.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How long does it take to become more flexible?</h3><p>Many people notice changes within a few weeks of daily practice, with more significant gains over several months.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is it possible to be too flexible?</h3><p>Yes. People who are naturally very flexible usually benefit more from strength and control work than from further stretching.</p></section><section id="keep-at-it"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Keep at It</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Flexibility rewards patience. A short daily practice, done calmly and consistently, is the most reliable way to move more freely.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Flexibility: How to Actually Improve Your Range of Motion', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-flexibility-and-mobility' AND `content` LIKE '%This is usually the most demanding part of the schedule physically, and%';

-- yoga-for-core-strength-and-stability
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">The core is not just the six-pack muscles. It is the whole system of muscles around the trunk, deep and surface, front, back and sides, that stabilises the spine and transfers force between the upper and lower body. Yoga trains it the way you actually use it: holding steady while everything else moves.</p><section id="what-the-core-is"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What the Core Really Is</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Your core includes the deep abdominal muscles, the obliques at the sides, the muscles along the spine, the glutes, the diaphragm and the pelvic floor. Its main job is not to bend you forward like a sit-up but to keep your trunk stable and transfer force efficiently.</p></section><section id="why-stability"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Why Stability Beats Crunches</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Crunches train one small part of the core in one direction. Real-life tasks, from carrying shopping to running, need the core to resist unwanted movement. Yoga&rsquo;s holds and balances train exactly that: staying steady, breathing calmly, while your arms and legs work.</p></section><section id="key-poses"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Key Poses for Core Strength</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Plank</strong> and forearm plank</li><li><strong>Side plank</strong> for the obliques</li><li><strong>Boat pose (Navasana)</strong> with knees bent to start</li><li><strong>Bird-dog</strong> from hands and knees</li><li><strong>Bridge pose</strong> for the glutes and back</li><li><strong>Locust pose (Salabhasana)</strong> for the back of the body</li><li><strong>Warrior III</strong> for stability on one leg</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Keep breathing. Holding your breath can make a pose feel more stable, but learning to stay steady while breathing is what transfers to real life.</p></div></section><section id="a-core-sequence"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A 15-Minute Core Sequence</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Bird-dog (8 slow reps each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Forearm plank (3 × 20 to 30 seconds)</strong></li><li><strong>Side plank, knee down if needed (20 seconds each side, twice)</strong></li><li><strong>Boat pose (3 × 15 seconds)</strong></li><li><strong>Locust pose (3 × 15 seconds)</strong></li><li><strong>Bridge (8 slow lifts)</strong></li><li><strong>Child&rsquo;s pose to finish</strong></li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Will yoga give me a six-pack?</h3><p>Yoga will strengthen your core, but visible abdominal muscles depend mostly on body fat levels, which come down to diet and overall activity.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is yoga good for back pain?</h3><p>Many people find core and mobility work helpful for general back stiffness. Back pain that is severe, persistent or comes with numbness or weakness should be checked by a doctor first.</p></section><section id="build-steadily"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Build Steadily</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Start with shorter holds and good form, then lengthen them over the weeks. A strong, steady core supports everything else you do.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>The core stabilises, it doesn&rsquo;t just crunch</li><li>Hold steady while breathing</li><li>Form first, then duration</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Core Strength: The Poses That Build Real Stability', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-core-strength-and-stability' AND `content` LIKE '%A mantra repeated with attention, again and again, becomes something clo%';

-- power-yoga-dynamic-yoga-fitness
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Studio timetables are full of names: power yoga, vinyasa flow, dynamic yoga, Ashtanga. They can look interchangeable, and in some ways they are. Here is what each term usually means, how they differ, and how to pick the right class for what you want.</p><section id="dynamic-yoga"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What &lsquo;Dynamic Yoga&rsquo; Means</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">&lsquo;Dynamic yoga&rsquo; is a broad term for any style where you move continuously from pose to pose, linking movement with breath, rather than holding each posture in stillness. Vinyasa flow and Ashtanga Vinyasa are dynamic styles.</p></section><section id="power-yoga"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What &lsquo;Power Yoga&rsquo; Means</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Power yoga is a fitness-focused style of dynamic yoga that grew out of Ashtanga. Classes tend to be vigorous, with more strength work, longer holds in demanding poses and less emphasis on chanting or philosophy. Sequences often vary from class to class.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Dynamic: any flowing, breath-linked style</li><li>Power: a vigorous, fitness-focused version</li><li>Ashtanga: a set traditional sequence</li></ul></div></section><section id="how-they-differ"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>How They Differ in Practice</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Intensity:</strong> power classes are usually the most demanding.</li><li><strong>Structure:</strong> Ashtanga follows a fixed sequence; vinyasa and power vary.</li><li><strong>Pace:</strong> flow classes keep moving; power classes may slow down for strength holds.</li><li><strong>Focus:</strong> power leans towards fitness; traditional dynamic styles often include more breathwork and meditation.</li></ul></section><section id="which-to-choose"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Which Should You Choose?</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>For a workout and strength:</strong> power yoga.</li><li><strong>For flowing movement and a moving meditation:</strong> vinyasa.</li><li><strong>For structure and progression:</strong> Ashtanga.</li><li><strong>For recovery or relaxation:</strong> neither; choose Hatha, Yin or restorative.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Names vary between studios. Read the class description or ask the teacher about the level and pace before you book.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is power yoga suitable for beginners?</h3><p>It can be, if the class is labelled beginner-friendly and you take options freely. Learning basic poses in a slower class first makes it easier and safer.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Does power yoga count as cardio?</h3><p>A vigorous class raises your heart rate and builds stamina, though steady running or cycling trains the heart and lungs more directly.</p></section><section id="does-it-matter"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Does It Matter?</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The label matters less than the class itself. Choose based on your goal, your fitness and how you feel afterwards. The best style is the one you will keep coming back to.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Power Yoga vs Dynamic Yoga: What''s the Difference and Does It Matter?', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'power-yoga-dynamic-yoga-fitness' AND `content` LIKE '%Traditional texts describe speech as one of the most constant, and most%';

-- yoga-for-womens-hormonal-balance
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Hormones influence energy, mood, sleep, appetite and cycles, and they are influenced in turn by how we live. Stress, poor sleep and irregular routines can all throw things off. Yoga will not rewrite your hormones, but it can help reset the everyday conditions they depend on.</p><section id="what-affects-hormones"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Affects Hormonal Balance</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Stress:</strong> long-term stress keeps cortisol elevated, which can affect sleep, appetite and cycles.</li><li><strong>Sleep:</strong> poor or irregular sleep disrupts several hormonal rhythms.</li><li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> irregular meals and very low intake can disrupt cycles.</li><li><strong>Activity:</strong> both too little and very intense exercise without enough fuel can play a role.</li><li><strong>Life stage:</strong> puberty, pregnancy, postpartum and perimenopause all bring natural shifts.</li></ul></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Supports Balance</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga&rsquo;s most direct effect is on stress and sleep. Slow breathing and relaxation help the body shift out of a constant stress state, and a calming evening practice can improve sleep. Regular gentle movement supports overall health, and the habit of checking in with your body helps you notice patterns in your cycle and energy.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Lower stress</li><li>Better sleep</li><li>Regular, moderate movement</li><li>Awareness of your own patterns</li></ul></div></section><section id="a-reset-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Daily Reset Routine</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Morning (5 minutes):</strong> gentle sun salutations and a few deep breaths in daylight.</li><li><strong>Afternoon (2 minutes):</strong> slow breathing with a longer exhale when stress builds.</li><li><strong>Evening (10 minutes):</strong> supported child&rsquo;s pose, reclined butterfly, legs up the wall and a short relaxation.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Keep regular sleep and meal times. Routine is one of the simplest supports for hormonal rhythms.</p></div></section><section id="cycle-aware"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Practising With Your Cycle</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Many women find their energy changes across the month. You might enjoy stronger practice in the first half of the cycle and prefer gentler, restorative practice in the days before and during your period. There is no fixed rule: notice what helps you.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga fix irregular periods?</h3><p>Yoga may help with stress-related changes, but irregular or absent periods can have many causes, including PCOS and thyroid problems, and should be checked by a doctor.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Which yoga poses help hormonal balance?</h3><p>No pose acts directly on a gland. Calming, restorative practices that reduce stress and improve sleep are what make the difference.</p></section><section id="when-to-see-a-doctor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to See a Doctor</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">See a doctor if you have very irregular, painful or heavy periods, missed periods, unexplained weight change, excess hair growth, severe mood changes or persistent fatigue. Yoga is a good companion to proper care, not a replacement for it.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Women''s Hormonal Balance: A Natural Reset Guide', `modified_date` = '2026-10-29'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-womens-hormonal-balance' AND `content` LIKE '%A sattvic rhythm usually begins before sunrise, with a quiet start rathe%';

